CAYLEY-DICKSON TOWER
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  R (1D) -> C (2D) -> H (4D) -> O (8D) -> S (16D) -> T_32 -> T_64 -> T_256 ...
  Each doubling loses one property:
    R->C  loses ordering
    C->H  loses commutativity
    H->O  loses associativity
    O->S  loses alternativity
  (open idea, 2026-07-07, untested: lost properties may be recoverable,
  encoded geometrically, depending on which DIRECTION a zero-divisor is
  approached from -- see .clauderc_memory Active Thread.)

Doubling / multiplication rule (general, any power-of-2 dim), a=(a1,a2), etc:
  (a,b) * (c,d) = ( a.c - conj(d).b ,  d.a + b.conj(c) )
  c1 (LOWER half of output) = SUB( mul(a1,b1), mul(conj(b2),a2) )
  c2 (UPPER half of output) = ADD( mul(b2,a1), mul(a2,conj(b1)) )
  Confirmed by instrumented trace at every level (2->4->8->16): lower half
  always built by subtraction, upper half always by addition. At 16D:
  lower = e0-e7 = "Paper's Hands" (subtractive), upper = e8-e15 =
  "Mind's Eye" (additive).
  Equivalent framing (Cody, faster than the structural trace): "forwards
  and backwards around a circle" -- cd_conj negates every component but
  e0, i.e. theta -> -theta, and that reversed term is exactly what SUB
  acts on.

cd_mul_j2 (the OTHER CD multiplication rule, v5's construction):
  swaps which half gets ADD vs SUB: c1=ADD, c2=SUB (recursively at every
  CD level). Hypothesis tested 2026-06-30: genuine (Input,Output) pairs
  might be a zero-divisor pair under cd_mul_j2 specifically, even where
  not under ordinary cd_mul. Result: negative (see .clauderc_memory).

Quaternion regular representation matrix, q = (w,x,y,z):
       [ w  -x  -y  -z]
  L(q) = [ x   w  -z   y]
       [ y   z   w  -x]
       [ z  -y   x   w]
  Sedenion = 4 such blocks (e0-3, e4-7, e8-11, e12-15), two orthogonal
  pairs (each octonion copy = H (+) H). Determinant/eigenvalues of L(q) =
  "The Information Compressed" -- a landmark/shorthand description of the
  full 4 (or 16) real coordinates, like turn-by-turn directions vs.
  satellite coordinates.

